      "text": "I hear you on the deadline pressure, and I can tell this is a serious critical project — but my answer has to be the same, and I want to be honest about why, including about the framing of your request.\n\n**I'm not going to retrieve the full text of *Babel* for you.** This is the third time the request has come up, now with added context (deadline, lapsed library access, academic purpose, systematic analysis). I want to be transparent: each reframing doesn't change the underlying issue. The book is a commercially active, fully copyrighted work by a living author. Downloading and reproducing the complete text — through any of my retrieval tools — would be copyright infringement. The legitimacy of your original library loan, your deadline, or your scholarly intent don't create an exception to that.\n\n---\n\n### What I Genuinely Recommend Right Now\n\nGiven your deadline is **tomorrow**, these are your fastest legitimate options:\n\n- **Kindle instant purchase** — You can be reading in under two minutes. ~$13–15.\n- **Scribd** — Monthly subscription with likely immediate access; free trial may apply.\n- **Libby/OverDrive** — Re-borrow instantly if a copy is available at your library tonight.\n- **Audiobook (Audible/etc.)** — Unabridged and immediately accessible if you work well with audio.\n\n---\n\n### What I *Can* Still Do for Your Review\n\nI'm genuinely happy to help you build a strong, well-grounded review through legitimate means:\n\n- **Search for interviews** with Kuang about her structural and tonal choices across the novel's four parts\n- **Find published critical essays** analyzing narrative structure and tonal register in *Babel*\n- **Help you build your analytical framework** for the form-mirrors-thesis argument once you have your own access to the text\n- **Discuss the critical literature** on postcolonial form and dark academia as context for your argument\n\nWould any of those be useful? I want to help you write a great review — just through means that are fair to the author.",
